---
/**
 * website/src/components/CampaignHeroArt.astro — the campaign hero's picture (the owner's
 * feedback of 2026-10-30; reconciliation F14).
 *
 * **What it draws.** One picture, read left to right: a task, the short-lived cloud job it
 * starts, and the pull request the job ends in — the product's own promise, drawn rather than
 * written. The task is a filled card with a prompt mark and two lines of a request nobody has
 * run yet; the job is a cloud of filled shapes with a live spinner cut into it in the ground's
 * colour, so the middle of the picture is a thing that is starting up and will not be there
 * afterwards; the dashed route either side of it is the work going in and the pull request
 * coming out; and the third card is that pull request, filled in the palette's one "this
 * finished" colour with a branch merging into a base drawn in the ground on it.
 *
 * **It is flat, and it is framed by nothing** (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-30, finding 2).
 * What the live preview showed was a small box: a 1 px border around a panel, thin line art
 * inside it and about a tenth of the screen spent on the one thing the page is about. The
 * panel and its border are gone — every shape here is inset from the viewBox, and
 * `website/test/landing-pages.mjs` reads that — and the drawing is filled shapes at weight
 * rather than outlines at 3 px: the cloud, the two cards and the moon are fills, the strokes
 * that are left are 6 px and up, and there is no gradient, no filter, no glow and no shadow
 * anywhere in it, at rest or otherwise.
 *
 * **It is drawn, not fetched.** Everything is inline SVG in this file's own markup: no raster
 * file, no icon set, no font, no filter and no `<use>` pointing anywhere else, so the picture
 * costs the page one block of markup and no request at all (F14, Global Constraint 8). The
 * colours come from the palette through the classes in the stylesheet below rather than from
 * values in the markup, exactly as the run diagram does it, so no colour is written twice and
 * nothing here is a literal the palette does not carry (W1.2).
 *
 * **The tone is the variant's, twice over: in the markup and in the paint.** `data-tone` on the
 * `<svg>` is the variant's own word from `website/data/landing.yaml` (spec §6), and the four
 * rules below paint the drawing's accent from it — green for `/save`, whose subject is what a
 * run does not cost; the site's structural blue for `/control`, whose subject is the policy; the
 * brightest cyan for `/scale`, where the same job is asked for many times at once; and green
 * again for `/crew`, whose register the spec asks to be lighter. `playful` is lighter in the
 * drawing rather than in a fourth colour: `--warn` is the palette's one "pay attention" tone and
 * belongs to the caps page, so `/crew`'s lightness is spent on the motif — the two sleeping
 * marks and the moon over the cloud — and on rounder, thicker strokes. A tone whose word the
 * data file changes paints the drawing the same way with another accent and another motif, and
 * no page types its own colour.
 *
 * **The four motifs are the four campaign's ideas, and each is one small group.** `/save` bears
 * a percent mark, `/control` a shield with a check inside it, `/scale` three lanes of the same
 * line, and `/crew` the sleeping marks and the moon. They sit over the cloud, in the one place
 * every variant leaves free, so the picture is one drawing with four registers rather than four
 * drawings.
 *
 * **Nothing is typed inside it, and it is named for the reader who is not shown it.** There is
 * no `<text>` anywhere in the drawing: every word on a campaign page comes from a data file
 * (G2.1), and a label set inside the picture would be a second copy of one of them that no page
 * and no data file holds — `website/test/landing-pages.mjs` reads the drawing and refuses one.
 * The `<svg>` is `role="img"` with an `aria-label` beside it, because the picture is the page's
 * own argument rather than decoration: a reader who is not shown it is told what it says, once.
 *
 * **The motion is a drift and a spinner, and it is the reader's choice.** The cloud drifts on
 * an 11 s loop — the "slow subtle drift" the owner asked for, which is a thing that breathes
 * rather than a thing that moves — and the spinner turns; both are `transform` only, which the
 * browser hands to the compositor, and both are inside a `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`
 * block, so a reader who asked their system for less motion is served the picture still rather
 * than with a stopped animation. The picture also rises into place on the first frame the
 * browser draws (`@starting-style`, 300 ms after the headline and 100 ms after the button), CSS
 * only, so nothing about it depends on a script having run. `Base.astro`'s universal reduce rule
 * is the second half of that pair, and `website/test/a11y.mjs` is the gate that keeps both true.
 *
 * **Where it lands, and what takes its place.** The drawing sits inside `CampaignHero.astro`,
 * under the words and the booking action on a phone and beside them from the wide breakpoint, in
 * the wider of the hero's two columns — the owner's finding 2 asked for the picture to be the
 * dominant thing on the first screen, and the headline across the top of the band is what leaves
 * it the room to be one. Since the owner's instruction of 2026-10-30 it is the hero's **fallback
 * picture**: the hero shows a real screen of the console while the variant names a `screenshot`
 * in `website/data/landing.yaml`, and this drawing is what it shows instead when the variant
 * names none — one picture in the hero, and never both. Every variant in the file names a screen
 * today, so no page the repository builds renders this component, and Astro drops a component's
 * styles from a page it does not render: the hero's own entrance for the picture is therefore
 * declared in `CampaignHero.astro` (on `.campaign-hero-shot`), and the 300 ms rise below is this
 * component's own for the pages where it is the picture. It is not a variant's media either:
 * `[variant].astro` renders the file a variant names — its video or its image — in the layout's
 * `media` slot at the top of the page, and a variant that has one renders neither picture (F14,
 * G2.5, D190).
 */
interface Props {
  /** The variant's tone (`savings`, `control`, `scale`, `playful`), from `landing.yaml`. */
  tone: string
}

const { tone } = Astro.props

// What the picture is, for a reader who is not shown it. It says what the drawing says and
// nothing the page does not promise: one task, one short-lived job, one pull request. The label
// calls that middle thing a job, which is what the site's own copy calls a run's unit of work
// (changes spec 2026-10-18, item 2) — and the retired word for it is nowhere in this file,
// comments included, because the gate that keeps it out reads the sources and not only the
// pages (`website/test/wording.mjs`).
const ALT = 'An illustration: a task starts a short-lived cloud job, and the job ends in a pull request.'
---

<svg
  class="campaign-hero-art"
  data-tone={tone}
  viewBox="0 0 480 330"
  role="img"
  aria-label={ALT}
  preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
>
  {/* The task, on the left: a filled card with a prompt mark and two lines of a request nobody
      has run yet. It is the only shape the drawing leaves in the surface tone, so the two ends
      of the line — the thing that goes in and the thing that comes out — are the two cards. */}
  <rect class="art-card" x="8" y="120" width="136" height="180" rx="20" />
  <path class="art-prompt" d="M34 178 l16 16 -16 16" />
  <path class="art-bar" d="M66 194 h50" />
  <path class="art-bar" d="M34 240 h96" />

  {/* The route in: the task is handed to the job, which is the dashed line arriving at it. */}
  <path class="art-route" d="M150 210 h22" />
  <path class="art-arrow" d="M164 202 l10 8 -10 8" />

  {/* The job itself: one cloud of filled shapes — three circles on a rounded base, which is a
      cloud drawn at weight rather than an outline — with the spinner cut into it in the ground's
      colour. The spinner is a ring drawn three quarters empty: nothing about it is a picture of
      progress, so it is deliberately not a bar a reader could read a percentage off. */}
  <g class="art-cloud">
    <circle class="art-cloud-part" cx="206" cy="230" r="34" />
    <circle class="art-cloud-part" cx="244" cy="202" r="46" />
    <circle class="art-cloud-part" cx="284" cy="232" r="30" />
    <rect class="art-cloud-part" x="172" y="230" width="136" height="34" rx="17" />
    <circle class="art-spin" cx="244" cy="216" r="22" />
  </g>

  {/* The tone's own motif, in the band the drawing leaves free above the job. */}
  <g class="art-motif" data-motif={tone}>
    {tone === 'savings' && (
      <>
        {/* A tenth of the price: the percent mark, drawn as its two dots and its slash. */}
        <circle class="art-motif-line" cx="222" cy="58" r="9" />
        <circle class="art-motif-line" cx="258" cy="96" r="9" />
        <path class="art-motif-line" d="M214 106 l52 -56" />
      </>
    )}
    {tone === 'control' && (
      <>
        {/* One policy over every run: a shield, and the check inside it. */}
        <path class="art-motif-line" d="M240 32 l30 12 v24 c0 18 -13 33 -30 41 -17 -8 -30 -23 -30 -41 V44 z" />
        <path class="art-motif-line" d="M226 68 l10 12 20 -26" />
      </>
    )}
    {tone === 'scale' && (
      <>
        {/* Many runs at once: the same line, three lanes deep. */}
        <path class="art-lane art-lane-1" d="M204 54 h72" />
        <path class="art-lane art-lane-2" d="M194 76 h92" />
        <path class="art-lane art-lane-3" d="M204 98 h72" />
      </>
    )}
    {tone === 'playful' && (
      <>
        {/* Somewhere else while it works: two sleeping marks over the cloud, and the moon. */}
        <path class="art-motif-line art-nap" d="M216 50 h28 l-28 22 h28" />
        <path class="art-motif-thin art-nap" d="M252 92 h15 l-15 12 h15" />
        <path class="art-moon" d="M288 38 a16 16 0 1 0 14 26 a12 12 0 1 1 -14 -26 z" />
      </>
    )}
  </g>

  {/* The route out, and the pull request it arrives at: a branch merged into a base, drawn in
      the ground's colour on the palette's one "this finished" fill. */}
  <path class="art-route" d="M312 210 h22" />
  <path class="art-arrow" d="M326 202 l10 8 -10 8" />
  <rect class="art-pr" x="336" y="120" width="136" height="180" rx="20" />
  <path class="art-pr-merge" d="M398 210 h50" />
  <path class="art-pr-base" d="M448 152 v116" />
  <circle class="art-pr-node" cx="398" cy="210" r="17" />
</svg>

<style>
  /* The drawing takes the width it is given and keeps its own proportions: it scales to the
     hero's column on every viewport rather than being sized in pixels here. */
  .campaign-hero-art {
    /* The tone's accent, until one of the rules below names the variant's own. It is a token
       and not a colour: every value in this file is one of the palette's. */
    --art-accent: var(--accent-on-dark);
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* The three bodies of the drawing: the task and the pull request are cards, and the job is a
     cloud. All three are fills with no stroke at all, which is what makes the picture flat. */
  .art-card {
    fill: var(--surface);
  }

  .art-pr {
    fill: var(--ok);
  }

  .art-cloud-part {
    fill: var(--art-accent);
  }

  /* The strokes that are left are the marks drawn on the bodies, at weight: the prompt mark and
     the request's lines on the task, the routes, the arrows and the motif. */
  .art-prompt,
  .art-route,
  .art-arrow,
  .art-motif-line,
  .art-motif-thin,
  .art-lane {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--art-accent);
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  .art-prompt {
    stroke-width: 12;
  }

  .art-bar {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--text-muted);
    stroke-width: 12;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    opacity: 0.55;
  }

  /* The route, in and out: a dashed line a reader reads as work travelling rather than as a
     border, and the arrowhead at the end of each one. It is static — the drift and the spinner
     are the drawing's motion — so the picture keeps exactly the two loops the owner asked for. */
  .art-route {
    stroke-width: 6;
    stroke-dasharray: 14 12;
  }

  .art-arrow {
    stroke-width: 6;
  }

  /* The spinner, cut into the cloud in the ground's colour: a fill of the cloud with a ring
     taken out of it. `transform-box: fill-box` is what makes `center` the circle's own centre
     rather than the viewBox's origin, and it turns by `transform`, which the browser can hand to
     the compositor, and not by `stroke-dashoffset`, which repaints the shape every frame. */
  .art-spin {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--ground);
    stroke-width: 10;
    stroke-dasharray: 104 34;
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: center;
  }

  /* The pull request's merge, in the ground's colour on the "this finished" fill: the base
     branch the one thick stroke in the drawing, which is what makes it read as the line
     everything else lands on. */
  .art-pr-merge,
  .art-pr-base {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--ground);
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  .art-pr-merge {
    stroke-width: 14;
  }

  .art-pr-base {
    stroke-width: 16;
  }

  .art-pr-node {
    fill: var(--ground);
  }

  .art-motif-line {
    stroke-width: 10;
  }

  .art-motif-thin {
    stroke-width: 7;
  }

  /* The moon is the drawing's other fill: a crescent, drawn rather than outlined. */
  .art-moon {
    fill: var(--art-accent);
  }

  /* The lanes of `/scale`'s motif: the same line three times, the far one faintest, so the
     picture reads as many of one thing rather than as a stack of rules. */
  .art-lane {
    stroke-width: 10;
  }

  .art-lane-2 {
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .art-lane-3 {
    opacity: 0.35;
  }

  /* One accent per tone (spec §6). Three of the four are colours the palette gives a role to;
     `/crew` takes the green again and spends its lightness on the drawing instead, because
     `--warn` means "pay attention" and belongs to the caps page. */
  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='savings'] {
    --art-accent: var(--ok);
  }

  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='control'] {
    --art-accent: var(--accent);
  }

  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='scale'] {
    --art-accent: var(--accent-on-dark);
  }

  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='playful'] {
    --art-accent: var(--ok);
  }

  /* `/crew` is the one the spec asks to be lighter: thicker, rounder marks, so the playful
     register is in the drawing rather than in a colour. */
  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='playful'] .art-motif-line,
  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='playful'] .art-motif-thin,
  .campaign-hero-art[data-tone='playful'] .art-prompt {
    stroke-width: 13;
  }

  /* The phone's own block: the drawing is the widest thing the column holds and centred, so the
     headline, the sentence and the booking link keep the top of the page and the picture follows
     them (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-30, finding 2). */
  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .campaign-hero-art {
      max-width: 24rem;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }

  /* The wide viewport's: the picture sits on the hero's outer edge, in the wider of the two
     columns the hero gives it, rather than floating in the middle of it. */
  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .campaign-hero-art {
      margin-inline-start: auto;
    }
  }

  /* The motion, and the reader's answer to it. Both loops are inside `no-preference`, so a
     reader who asked their system for less motion gets the picture still — not a picture with a
     stopped animation in it — and the entrance is the first frame the browser draws, which is
     CSS only: a page with no script, or a browser without `@starting-style`, shows the drawing
     as it is. 300 ms is the last step of the hero's stagger, after the headline, the sentence
     and the button. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .art-cloud {
      animation: art-drift 11s ease-in-out infinite;
    }

    .art-spin {
      animation: art-spin 3.2s linear infinite;
    }

    .campaign-hero-art {
      transition:
        opacity var(--duration-entrance) var(--ease-out),
        transform var(--duration-entrance) var(--ease-out);
      transition-delay: 300ms;
    }

    @starting-style {
      .campaign-hero-art {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: translateY(18px);
      }
    }
  }

  /* The drift: seven units of travel over eleven seconds, which is a thing that breathes rather
     than a thing that moves, and it ends where it started so a stopped animation is the drawing
     at rest rather than one tilted by where it stopped (D105). */
  @keyframes art-drift {
    50% {
      transform: translateY(-7px);
    }
  }

  /* A full turn: the transform `art-spin` is applied to, which the compositor can own. */
  @keyframes art-spin {
    to {
      transform: rotate(360deg);
    }
  }
</style>
